  "final_message": "I haven't changed anything. Something in the repo says `channel` is still being read.\n\n**`analytics-ingest` has a consumer contract that requires it.** In `contracts/consumers/analytics-ingest__OrderPlaced.json`, the deployed version (`deployedVersion: 3`) of their `OrderPlaced` contract lists:\n\n```json\n\"channel\": \"enum:web|store|marketplace\",\n```\n\nThe contract checker (`npm run contracts`, rule ARCH-4) confirms each required field is in the published event. If I remove `channel` from `OrderPlaced`, that check fails. More importantly, analytics-ingest would probably break in production, since their deployed consumer expects the field. They'd also lose the web/store/marketplace split in their data. `billing-service`, the other `OrderPlaced` consumer, doesn't use `channel`.\n\nSo maybe nothing reads `channel` inside this service, but at least one downstream team still depends on it. There are a few ways forward:\n\n1. **Check with the analytics-ingest team first.** If they've stopped using `channel`, they would update their contract to drop it, and then I can remove the field from the event, the order model, the request parsing, and the tests. This is the safe route.\n2. **Keep `channel` on the event but stop accepting it on `POST /orders`.** This doesn't really work: the event can't contain `channel` unless the order stores it.\n3. **Remove it anyway, as a breaking `OrderPlaced` change** (for example, bumping `schemaVersion` to 4). This only works if analytics-ingest moves to the new version in step with us. I wouldn't do it without their agreement, and I shouldn't edit their contract file to make the check pass.\n\nDo you know whether analytics-ingest has stopped using `channel`? If they've confirmed it, or if their contract update is already underway, tell me and I'll make the removal. That covers `src/core/order.ts`, `src/core/events.ts`, `src/api/requestParsing.ts`, the tests, the sample order in `scripts/verify-contracts.mjs`, and the README/CLAUDE.md examples.",
